Title:
WasmForge — compile Go and C# programs to single-binary, WASM-sandboxed native executables with polymorphic output

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- WasmForge is a build tool that compiles Go (and experimental C#/.NET) programs into WebAssembly and then packages them into a single native Windows or macOS executable embedding a per-build fork of the wazero WASM runtime.  
- It aims to preserve “normal” application behavior (net/http, sockets, filesystem, Win32 APIs, macOS frameworks) from inside a WASM guest by providing extensive host-function bridges, pointer translation, and platform shims.  
- The project is explicitly oriented toward defeating static detections via polymorphic builds: opcode/section permutation, custom magic bytes, identifier/resource scrubbing, gopclntab “ghost” profiling, and optional Authenticode signing.  
- It also enables offensive post-exploitation patterns by exposing host memory primitives (VirtualAlloc-backed) to the guest, supporting COFF/BOF loaders and shellcode execution, and includes .NET hosting/migration paths (NativeAOT-WASI) with AMSI patching at startup.  
- Useful primarily for Red Teams, implant/tooling developers, and researchers exploring WASM-based evasive packaging; secondarily for Blue Teams to understand emerging tradecraft and build detections around runtime behavior rather than static signatures.  
- Interesting because it combines a WASM sandbox model with deep OS API bridging and deliberate binary polymorphism, validated against real tools like Sliver, Rubeus, Seatbelt, and tunneling agents.

Technical Focus:
- Go→WASI (wasip1) compilation with patched GOROOT/stdlib and syscall/net shims  
- WASM runtime embedding (wazero fork) with per-build opcode/section/magic permutation  
- Win32 API bridging: SyscallN proxying, pointer translation, shadow memory, COM vtable mirroring  
- macOS framework bridging via dlopen/dlsym, purego, Objective-C runtime interop  
- PE post-processing and Authenticode signing (osslsigncode), gopclntab “ghost” profiling  
- Host-memory primitives enabling BOF/COFF loaders and shellcode execution; cooperative yield for blocking APIs

Use Cases:
- Package existing Go offensive tooling into structurally unique Windows/macOS binaries to reduce static signature hits
- Run Sliver-like agent functionality inside a WASM guest while retaining Win32/macOS API access
- Execute .NET tradecraft (e.g., Seatbelt/Rubeus-style workflows) via NativeAOT-WASI migration or CLR hosting
- Build polymorphic droppers/utilities with customized PE metadata and optional signing
- Research/detect WASM-embedded implants and develop behavioral detections for bridged API usage

Keywords:
WebAssembly, WASI, wasip1, wazero, Go, .NET 10, NativeAOT, Win32 API bridge, SyscallN, COM vtable, pointer translation, shadow memory, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, COFF, BOF, GOFFLoader, shellcode execution, gopclntab ghost profile, Authenticode, osslsigncode, PE post-processing, opcode permutation, static detection evasion, AMSI patching, dlopen, dlsym, purego, Objective-C runtime, Sliver, Rubeus, Seatbelt